        },
        "end": {
          "line": 123,
          "character": 2
        }
      },
      "title": "The FastAPI app and its CORS list",
      "description": "**New idea — FastAPI:** a Python web framework. Decorators like `@app.post(\"/chat/stream\")` attach functions to addresses, and it reads JSON bodies into typed models for you.\n\n**How it works:** the CORS list allows your production domains, localhost, and any `adagent-ai…vercel.app` preview (`allow_origin_regex`).\n\n**Why it matters:** the browser calls this server directly, from another address, so CORS is required here, unlike the API inside Next.js.\n\n**Watch out:** `allow_methods` and `allow_headers` are `*`, which is permissive. That's acceptable only because every endpoint checks the token itself."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat_server.py",
      "line": 136,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 130,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 136,
          "character": 35
        }
      },
      "title": "The request's shape",
      "description": "**New idea — Pydantic models:** a class that describes the expected JSON. FastAPI checks incoming data against it and rejects bad requests with a 422 error.\n\n**How it works:** a chat request has a required `message`, plus optional `user_id`, `organization_id`, `history` and `context`.\n\n**Why it matters:** the endpoint can trust the *shape* of the data, though not its truth (next steps).\n\n**Watch out:** `history` and `context` are loose (`list`, `dict`), so their insides aren't checked."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat_server.py",
      "line": 208,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 168,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 208,
          "character": 6
        }
      },
      "title": "The chat endpoint",
      "description": "**How it works:**\n1. `validate_user_session` works out who you are from the token. Failure means 401.\n2. Organisation comes from the body or the header.\n3. Your settings (model, context mode, enabled accounts) come from `context`.\n4. It returns a `StreamingResponse` that runs `stream_chat_response` and sends each event as it's produced.\n\n**Why it matters:** this is the one door into the agent. Everything in tours 07–12 starts here.\n\n**Watch out:** `X-Accel-Buffering: no` tells proxies like Nginx not to hold the response back. Without it, a proxy could buffer the whole answer and the streaming would be lost."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/utils/providers.py",
      "line": 74,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 40,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 74,
          "character": 20
        }
      },
      "title": "Asking the API who you are",
      "description": "**How it works:** take the token from `x-stack-access-token` or `Authorization: Bearer`, then send it to the API's `/api/chat/internal/validate-token` with the shared secret. A valid answer gives your user id.\n\n**Why it matters:** the agent trusts the API's answer, not anything the browser claims, such as `user_id` in the body.\n\n**Watch out:** this costs one extra network call per message. If `INTERNAL_API_KEY` isn't set, it returns `None` and every chat gets a 401, a common local setup mistake."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/streaming/processor.py",
      "line": 171,
      "selection": {
