          "line": 171,
          "character": 35
        }
      },
      "title": "Setting up a stream",
      "description": "**New idea — async generator:** `stream_chat_response` uses `yield` to hand out SSE events one at a time, and FastAPI sends each one as soon as it's yielded.\n\n**How it works:** it makes a short stream id, starts tracking metrics, and sends the id first so the browser could reconnect. Then it creates two queues:\n- `content_queue` for text pieces from the AI model\n- `output_queue` for finished SSE events, in the order they should go out\n\n**Why it matters:** the graph runs in the background and drops things into the queues. This function just reads `output_queue` and sends.\n\n**Watch out:** the id is only the first 8 characters of a UUID. It's also used as the LangGraph thread id, so the same short id identifies the saved state."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/streaming/processor.py",
      "line": 239,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 173,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 239,
          "character": 41
        }
      },
      "title": "Running the graph",
      "description": "**How it works:** it loops over everything `run_graph` yields (tour 07):\n- an `__interrupt__` means a tool needs approval, so it sends `tool_approval_required`\n- anything else is a node's update, converted into SSE events by `_convert_state_to_sse`\n\nAt the end it sends stop signals (`None`) down both queues.\n\n**Why it matters:** this is the bridge between LangGraph's world (state updates) and the browser's world (events).\n\n**Watch out:** it waits for `content_done` before stopping the output queue, so no text piece is lost or arrives after `done`."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/streaming/processor.py",
      "line": 254,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 241,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 254,
          "character": 31
        }
      },
      "title": "Text pieces become events",
      "description": "**How it works:** it reads each text piece from `content_queue`, keeps a copy, and wraps it as a `content` event on `output_queue`. `None` means stop.\n\n**Why it matters:** the specialist (tour 09) puts text into the queue word by word. This is how those words reach your screen while the model is still writing.\n\n**Watch out:** two producers write to `output_queue`: this task and the graph task. That's why everything funnels through one queue, so the order stays right."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/streaming/processor.py",
      "line": 278,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 256,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 278,
          "character": 25
        }
      },
      "title": "The main loop",
      "description": "**New idea — tasks:** `asyncio.create_task` starts a function running in the background while this one carries on.\n\n**How it works:** both workers start. The loop waits up to 0.1 seconds for the next event, yields it, and repeats. On a timeout it checks whether both workers are finished and drains what's left.\n\n**Why it matters:** this keeps the stream moving without busy-waiting, and stops cleanly when the work is done.\n\n**Watch out:** the 0.1-second timeout is a polling check, not a delay you'll notice."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/streaming/processor.py",
      "line": 314,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 289,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 314,
          "character": 62
        }
      },
      "title": "Always finishing cleanly",
      "description": "**How it works:** in `finally` (which runs no matter what), it cancels any running work, marks the stream finished, saves usage metrics (tokens, cost, time), then sends `finished` and `done`.\n\n**Why it matters:** the browser always gets `done`, so the spinner always stops.\n\n**Watch out:** if the browser disconnects, this cleanup cancels the graph task, so the run stops part-way. There's no background completion for a closed tab."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/streaming/processor.py",
      "line": 378,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 362,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 378,
          "character": 16
        }
      },
      "title": "From node updates to events",
      "description": "**How it works:** each node's update becomes events the UI understands:\n- **router** → `routing` and `agent`\n- **entity loader** → `action_required` (for example, connect AdMob first)\n- **specialist** → `thinking`, `content`, `tool` (one per tool call), or `result` when there are no tool calls\n- **tool executor** → `tool_result`, `tool_denied`, `action_required`\n\n**Why it matters:** this table is the contract between the agent and the chat UI. Tour 12 shows the other side.\n\n**Watch out:** it skips thinking or text that was already streamed live (`thinking_streamed`, `content_streamed`), so you never see the same words twice."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/streaming/events.py",
      "line": 27,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 9,
