        },
        "end": {
          "line": 27,
          "character": 18
        }
      },
      "title": "The list of event types",
      "description": "**What it is:** every event the agent can send, defined in one place.\n\n**How it works:** each event is a small Pydantic class (below this list), and `format_sse` at the bottom of the file turns one into the text `data: {...}` followed by a blank line.\n\n**Why it matters:** the frontend's `StreamEvent` type in `lib/api.ts` (tour 04) mirrors this list. Change one and you must change the other.\n\n**Watch out:** `TASK_PROGRESS`, `TASK_COMPLETE` and `TASK_ERROR` exist for background tasks, but the main chat flow doesn't send them."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/streaming/state.py",
      "line": 23,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 11,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 23,
          "character": 31
        }
      },
      "title": "Where live state is kept",
      "description": "**How it works:** which streams are active is written to JSON files in the server's temp folder. Results waiting to be collected sit in a Python dict in memory. Approvals (tour 11) use temp files the same way.\n\n**Why it matters:** it's simple and works on one server.\n\n**Watch out:** this is a single-server design. With two server instances, one wouldn't see the other's files or memory, and a restart wipes it all. The graph's own state is safer, because it's saved in Postgres (tour 07)."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat_server.py",
